{
  "id": "Compound::DB01206 - Disease::DOID:1319",
  "verbalization": "Lomustine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Brain Cancer through a series of interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Lomustine causes Dysplasia, which is then treated by Carmustine, and both are part of the N-Nitrosoureas class of compounds. This implies a potential role of Lomustine in the development of Brain Cancer, possibly through its alkylating activity, which is supported by its inclusion in Alkylating Activity and its relationship with Procarbazine. Procarbazine, in turn, is used to treat Brain Cancer, indicating a broader therapeutic context. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Lomustine to contribute to the development of Brain Cancer via its alkylating properties, although the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence underscores the importance of N-Nitrosoureas in the treatment and pathogenesis of Brain Cancer, suggesting a possible repositioning of Lomustine in oncology therapies.",
